#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""herdr-sleeper plugin — sleep idle resumable agents in Herdr panes, wake them later.

A Herdr plugin (v1 manifest) grown from the standalone `scripts/herdr-sleeper`
reference implementation; the fail-closed safety rules are carried over
unchanged, and the plugin adds what only a plugin can do:

  * an in-process idle watcher (no launchd): started by the `startup` hook,
    polled from `agent list`, idle clocked by `state_change_seq` movement —
    no transcript mtime, so every agent kind with a session ref is eligible
  * wake on focus (`pane.focused`), wake by pressing Enter in the pane (a
    wake stub is left running there), and wake by command
  * slept panes keep their sidebar row (`pane report-agent` claim) with a
    `<kind> · sleeping` display label

Exit path per agent kind: SIGTERM for claude and opencode (both shut down
cleanly; a draft in the composer is discarded, never submitted — this is why
the standalone script's composer-empty check only applies to kinds exited by
typing into the composer), a typed command for anything else.

Usage (normally via `herdr plugin action invoke djbclark.herdr-sleeper.*`):

  herdr-sleeper startup                 ensure the watcher (idempotent)
  herdr-sleeper sleep-pane [PANE]       manual sleep (context pane, or arg)
  herdr-sleeper wake [PANE|NAME]        wake one (default: context pane) / --all
  herdr-sleeper list                    what is sleeping (and what is orphaned)
  herdr-sleeper log                     events, newest last
  herdr-sleeper on-focus                pane.focused hook (internal)
  herdr-sleeper stub PANE UUID          in-pane wake stub (internal)
  herdr-sleeper scan                    one watcher tick, on demand
  herdr-sleeper stop / start            stop the watcher and keep it stopped / undo that
  herdr-sleeper deliver TARGET --from SENDER   message on stdin to a live or sleeping agent (wakes it)
  herdr-sleeper presence [--json]       live and sleeping agents, for agents looking for peers
  herdr-sleeper claude-hook             Claude Code hook: ListAgents shows sleepers, SendMessage wakes them
  herdr-sleeper uninstall               stop the watcher, wake everything, report what is left

Fail-closed rules, inherited from the standalone script and its three review
rounds, the live-deployment iteration, and the v0.1.1 review (Fable, Grok):

1. Eligible only when: a supported agent kind with a session id (and, for
   claude, an on-disk transcript — nothing to resume otherwise); Herdr
   status idle/done; pane not focused; not excluded; session id not open in
   another pane; idle for the configured window; original argv safely
   replayable (`--fork-session`, `--print`, `--session-id`, positional
   prompts, anything after `--`, options of unknown arity → refuse).
2. Everything is re-checked after the journal write and immediately before
   the exit, including that `state_change_seq` and the session id have not
   moved; the exit targets the pane id, never the name; "exited" needs
   Herdr to drop the agent *and* process-info to show no agent process —
   unreadable process-info is unknown, not gone.
3. The record is written *before* the exit. If the agent is still there
   after the wait it stays `exit-requested`; the next tick reconciles
   (dropped only after two consecutive sightings with a real process, or
   when the pane runs a different session; `asleep` once verifiably gone;
   `displaced` when our own wake put another session there — by hand only).
   A record is never deleted because its pane vanished; the manual resume
   command is printed instead.
4. Pane identity. A changed `terminal_id` alone is a Herdr restart (restore
   re-allocates every terminal id and re-applies the saved 💤 label), not
   reuse: same pane id + our 💤 label + a bare shell wakes, and the new
   terminal id is recorded only with a wake that succeeded. A new terminal
   without the label (workspace ids restart at max(restored)+1, so a fresh
   pane can get a recycled id in the same cwd), or one running another
   session, is a reused pane id: the record is re-keyed `orphan:<uuid8>`
   (phase `orphaned`, one event), never auto-woken, listed with its manual
   resume line. A legacy record with no terminal id needs the label too. A
   record is dropped only when the *same* terminal now runs a different
   session (deliberate). cwd is a wake precondition ("cd back"), not identity.
5. Wake refuses if the session id is live in any pane or any process that
   selects it (our own stub is matched by argv structure, not substring),
   when that cannot be verified (an empty process list is unknown), when an
   `exit-requested` agent is still there (reconcile's two-sighting rule
   decides), or when the pane moved to another cwd. Recovered argv goes
   through the same replay filter, in `wake` and in the stub. The stub never
   pops its record: under the lock, after the last checks, it marks it
   `waking` with its pid (exec keeps the pid); every other path refuses a
   `waking` record whose pid is alive; reconcile finishes it once the session
   runs (and gives the agent its name back) or puts it back to `asleep` if the
   pid died; anything that stops the stub before the exec puts it back at
   once, to an orphan key if the pane's record names another session by then.
   `sleep-pane` never writes over a record kept for another session.
6. A non-object state file, malformed config, unknown keys, booleans where
   numbers go, conflicting env aliases, or a `nan`/negative/`inf` window
   disables sleeping (logged every tick) — `wake`/`list`/`log` keep working.
   A file lock serialises overlapping runs; the journal is re-read under it
   before every write.
7. The watcher never gives up: a failed tick (any exception) costs one tick
   and backs off to at most 10 min; one watcher per state dir (its own
   lock); SIGTERM finishes the current transaction first; any plugin
   activity respawns a missing watcher, or replaces one older than the
   watcher lock. Idle clocks persist in `idle.json` keyed pane + terminal id
   + `state_change_seq` + session (wall clock), written before any sleep of
   the tick; anything missing, damaged, or mismatched starts the clock now —
   a later sleep, never an earlier one; `scan` honours the same clocks.

Ideas adopted from the two community plugins studied before writing this
(dalogax/herdr-agent-hibernate, prabhatgmp/herdr-park): the SIGTERM exit
path, the seq-based idle clock, wake-on-focus with a per-pane wake lock,
`terminal_id` as the pane identity that survives pane-id reuse, the sidebar
claim, the in-pane Enter stub, and `notification show` with freed-MB
reporting. What this plugin keeps that they do not: the journal state
machine above, argv replay safety, session-live-elsewhere refusal on wake,
config validation, and damaged-state quarantine.
"""

